:: item.SacredOpalListBook [static-storage]
{=
(metadata: 
	"id", [The Many Sacred Opals and Their Human Creators],
    "key", (a: "document", "book",),
    "value", (a: 70, 0, 0, 0, 0, 20,),
    "img", "https://fallenlondon.wiki/w/images/3/33/Bookbrown.png",
    "desc", [A hefty record of the most notable achievements in magical science within the last ten centuries of Nurandah, published by the Bakho University Press. Will your name be in such a book one day?],
    "tip", [Envy is a form of leisure.],
    "gainText", [You are sure that you have come across some version of this book before. Books like this can be found on the shelves of every university city, both a source of motivation and envy for all budding magi.],
    "lossText", [As you part ways with the book, you tell yourself that your name will be listed in the next edition, or the next, or the next.],
    "content", [{

<!--elipses-->
(set: _e to [$cssDesc[`[...]`]])

(set: _SacredOpal to (a:
[<!--1-->
In the year 665 AD, ''Farrah of House Mavkhan'', journeying with certification from ''Arokhsar University of Mahzana'', successfully synthesized and defended her first Sacred Opal at the age of twenty-nine, earning her the recognition as "master" among her peers.
<br>
_e
<br>
Having amassed a small fortune, along with a small loan from her mercantile relatives, Farrah-Mavkhan was able to mobilize a force of a fifty alchemists and artificers, paid anywhere from thirty to eighty rials per day's work.
<br>
_e
<br>
Each collaborator was assigned their own narrow spectrum of the Sacred Opal to synthesize, working side-by-side for two years in a rental warehouse to assemble Farrah-Mavkhan's Sacred Opal.
<br>
_e
<br>
Five of the original fifty perished in the process - two from laboratory accidents, two from health complications relating to excessive elemental exposure, and one from old age. Still, the project expanded, and by the end, there were close to a hundred collaborators.
<br>
_e
<br>
Farrah-Mavkhan was not the first to theorize an industrialized approach to Sacred Opal synthesis, but she was the first to put it into practice at such a scale.
<br>
_e
<br>
Although her model was shown to be economically unsustainable - with the initial capital and subsequent labor cost far exceeding the contemporary relative value of a Sacred Opal - it was an important step in the eventual emergence of modern-day large-scale magical production.
<br>
_e
<br>
A motion was adopted to limit subsequent journeymen from submitting Sacred Opal which they did not synthesize by themselves, although what constitutes "by themselves" is still a subject of much debate.
<br>
_e
],

[<!--2-->
In the year 540 AD, ''Sasar of House Nuran'', elector-prince of then-autonomous Esora, journeying with certification from the ''Illustrious Esora Davehkhan'', successfully synthesized and defended his first Sacred Opal at the age of fifty-four, earning him the recognition as "master" among his peers.
<br>
_e
<br>
Already a generous patron before entering academia, Sasar-Nuran was much welcomed by a faction of loyalist davehmar, who shared with him a wish to restore the historical bond between Esora and Nurandah.
<br>
_e
<br>
Sasar-Nuran worked tirelessly to prove the superiority of imperial technology, which he - a newcomer to magic - used to win a wager against and to humiliate Esora Davehkhan's sovereigntist provost by producing a Sacred Opal before the latter did.
<br>
_e
<br>
Although the magical technique Sasar-Nuran employed were not considered breakthrough in Nurandah at the time, and it was revealed a century later that he was covertly receiving considerable help from imperial advisors, this stunt proved pivotal in tipping the stalemate in Esora's loyalists-versus-sovereigntists debate.
<br>
_e
<br>
Now master, Sasar-Nuran and his loyalist faction spent the next two decades consolidating power among the local davehmars, gaining a majority in Esora's electoral college, and bringing the once-independent territory back into Nurandah's fold.
<br>
_e
<br>
For his deeds, Sasar-Nuran was awarded an honorary chair in the imperial council of ministers. He oversaw the purge of sovereigntists in Esora's academies, including his alma mater.
<br>
_e
],

[<!--3-->
In the year 767 AD, ''Marsit of South Zagash'', better known by his mononym ''Marsit'', journeying with certification from the ''Zagash School of Engineering Sciences'', successfully synthesized and defended his first Sacred Opal at the age of seventeen, earning him the recognition as "master" among his peers.
<br>
_e
<br>
Born under the Red Sign, Marsit developed a rare affinity to vehzin, earning him the nickname of "Red Steel Charmer" and a reputation as a local child prodigy. Still, his early life was beset by poverty, which forced him to survive as a wandering tinkerer-for-hire.
<br>
_e
<br>
Eventually, Marsit caught the attention of a wealthy aristocrat, who used his influence to urge the Zagash School of Engineering Sciences admit the young boy, promising to sponsor the entirety of Marsit's tuition with the condition that Marsit must complete his apprenticeship in five years instead of the standard twelve. Otherwise, the sponsorship would turn into debt.
<br>
_e
<br>
Two years into his apprenticeship, Marsit was already developing an original design of a last-stage Opal assembler made entirely out recycled parts from discarded school equipment.
<br>
_e
<br>
Although Marsit initially wanted to keep the project a secret, paranoid that the administrators would claim his invention for themselves, his dormmates eventually convinced him to publicize the invention.
<br>
_e
<br>
The Zagash School's council, under much scrutiny, agreed to recognize Marsit's machine - the so-called "junk synthesizer" which managed to produce around five grains of Sacred Opal before burning out - and to award him a deferred master's degree, since Marsit was not yet a journeyman. It would take him another four years to complete his apprenticeship and claim his title.
<br>
_e
<br>
In order to pay off his debt, Marsit joined his former sponsor's workshop. There, he further refined his machine, far surpassing his initial submission. The workshop increased its profits and scale exponentially within a few years, partly due to this proprietary cost-saving technology, partly due to Marsit's celebritydom.
<br>
_e
<br>
When Marsit passed away from a vehzin poisoning, aged thirty two, a will in his name emerged, indicating his wish to transfer all of his research notes and savings, though the latter amounted to only around three hundred rials, to his employer and once-sponsor.
<br>
_e
<br>
During the social reforms following the Grand Exchange's Mutiny, many private enterprises were dissolved and brought under direct imperial control. Among these was Marsit's former employer.
<br>
_e
<br>
As a result, details of Marsit's later inventions was disseminated among the wider scientific community, sparking an "arms race" to see who could produce the least costly Opal-capable synthesizer.
<br>
_e
<br>
],

[<!--4-->
In the year 409 AD, ''Zinzan of House Padimar'', satmar of the Kudkuch Legion, journeying with certification from the ''Kudkuch Academy'', successfully synthesized and defended their first Sacred Opal at the age of thirty-five, earning them the recognition as "master" among his peers.
<br>
_e
<br>
Within Nurandah's many martial traditions, few were were than sixth-century Kudkuch, and among that province's martial class, few went through regimens more exacting than graduates of the Kudkuch Academy.
<br>
_e
<br>
Like many younger scions of the Great Noble Houses (which today are better known as the Kuchkhan League), Zinzan underwent neuterization prior to joining the military, forfeiting any rival claim to their older siblings' inheritance.
<br>
_e
<br>
During their journeying years, Zinzan quickly rose through the ranks of the Kudkuch Legion and became a satmar - commander of a hundred-strong company. Their legendary endurance and reputation for gentleness earned them the moniker "Mother", and their soldiers, all admirers, began to refer to themselves by a derivative nickname too rude for print.
<br>
_e
<br>
Zinzan led their company in successful skirmishes against the Keshan Empire to the north, during what is now known as the First Keshan War. After recovering a lost tome from a defeated serpent-saint, Zinzan discovered a method to create Sacred Opals using the magus's own body as the synthesis forge - the so-called "body magic".
<br>
_e
<br>
Zinzan withdrew from their post and spent the next three years in isolation. A former comrade who came to visit describe their former commander as "more bark than skin, more stone than flesh".
<br>
_e
<br>
Eventually, Zinzan emerged from hiding, miraculously pregnant. Hospitalers reported hearing heartbeats, but when it was time to deliver the child, a fist of Sacred Opal was birthed instead.
<br>
_e
<br>
From their birthing bed, Zinzan defened their method before a davehmar council, staying lucid until the final judgment. They passed away that afternoon from the parting's blood loss. A hundred men attended their wake.
<br>
_e
<br>
But the tomb was empty. Zinzan's body was taken and autopsied by Sisua hospitalers, who discovered that the bark-like skin was due to callousing, perhaps from excessive and exotic physical training during their years of isolation.
<br>
_e
<br>
Few ever managed to replicate Zinzan's feat, and those who did could not survive the ordeal. Their findings contributed greatly to our modern physical cultivation practices, including meditation. Neuterization briefly rose among the aristocratic houses, perhaps driven to a superstitious desire to recreate another "champion".
<br>
_e
<br>
The "child" is currently displayed in a hallway in the imperial palace in Nudankhan.
<br>
_e
<br>
],

[<!--5-->
In the year 229 AD, ''Atras of House Vehkhan'', journeying with certification from the 'Nudankhan Council'', successfully synthesized and defended his first Sacred Opal at the age of twenty-two, earning him the recognition as "master" among his peers.
<br>
_e
<br>
Atras-Vehkhan was the first magus to demonstrate the conservation hypothesis of Sacred Opals.
<br>
_e
<br>
The conservation hypothesis stated that Sacred Opals are only unique in their molar arrangement and did not constitute a novel substance. It followed that an Opal could be decomposed back into the exact molar amount of elements that were originally used in its synthesis.
<br>
_e
<br>
Failing to demonstrate conservation meant that the central dogma of magic - that all things were made of only five elements - would come into question.
<br>
_e
<br>
Prior attempts to confirm this hypothesis never went beyond ideation, for they were met with a dual problem.
<br>
_e
<br>
One, measurement instruments at the time were inexact, and so a large enough - around two hundred grains - Opal must be used in order to escape the margin of error.
<br>
_e
<br>
Two, there was no safe enough containment method to house the resultant output if the stone was any larger than one grain. Whereas synthesis input could be done gradually, decomposition output was an exponential cascade.
<br>
_e
<br>
In an act of, according to his contemporaries, "profound suicidality", Atras-Vehkhan volunteered his own mind as the housing chamber.
<br>
_e
<br>
Emperor Firsa III used his authority as yek-davehmar to override his own council, approving the experiment on the condition that Atras-Vehkhan would perform in an empty auditorium, with judges observing from behind warded glass, and that House Vehkhan would pay for the to-be-destroyed Opal, the magic glass, the red steel reinforcements, the warding rituals, and any damages that might occur.
<br>
_e
<br>
Atras-Vehkhan convinced his parents to agree by lying to them about the nature of the experiment.
<br>
_e
<br>
The result was within a 0.0001 molar error - a clear demonstration of the conservation hypothesis.
<br>
_e
<br>
Atras-Vehkhan immediately succumbed to complex elemental sickness, falling on the auditorium's floor. Shockingly, instead of showing any outward sign of magical disintegration, his body remained intact. The elements had formed a lattice that suspended the expected magical cascade.
<br>
_e
<br>
In the hallway, his fellow researchers began reading aloud Atras-Vehkhan's defense, which he had prepared prior to the demonstration along with answers for two hundred possible questions from the judges.
<br>
_e
<br>
To fulfill the traditional requirement for graduation - a Sacred Opal - the defense submitted Atras-Vehkhan's own corpse in lieu of the stone, arguing that reversing the decomposition would have been trivial had Atras-Vehkhan survived.
<br>
_e
<br>
This boldness impressed Emperor Firsa III greatly, and he decided, once again overriding his council, to award Atras-Vehkhan with a posthumous master's degree. His fellow researchers were expelled and imprisoned on charges of impersonation and plagiarism.
<br>
_e
<br>
Atras-Vehkhan's body was interred in a mausoleum located in faraway Askava instead his family's necropolis in the capital due to concerns of the elemental lattice one day failing and leaking into the surrounding area. Both the mausoleum and Atras-Vehkhan's body were lost during the Charlatan's Rebellion fifty years later.
<br>
_e
<br>
],

))

<!--?currentEntry: Where the book's content is displayed-->
[
<!--if reading has initialized (indicated by $stuff having a "readingorder")-->
(if: $stuff contains "readingOrder")[(print: _SacredOpal's ($stuff's readingOrder's ($stuff's readingTurn)))]

<!--else, preface-->
(else:)[{
''Preface''
<br>
The Khur-a-Mah, the Sun-and-Moon, the Matter-Of-All-Things, the Magic Stone: the Sacred Opal has many names, just as it does many lights and many miraculous possbilities.
<br>
The Opal is at once the highest achievement in magic and also a stepping stone to heights yet greater. This seeming paradox is reflected in its composition: The greatest wonder of the natural world is that it exists, that all primeaval constituent parts - At, Ab, Gor, Rokh, and Zam - were what they were, prior to their coalescence into all phenomena.
<br>
By whichever means, when a magus produces a Sacred Opal, they also demonstrate that they were able to grasp the essential state of the universe, and from this first principle, they are fully ready to venture into any frontier, model any phenomena, and produce any object, for there is nothing that is not of this world. The magus is then a //master//.
<br>
However, much like how a literate person is in theory capable of writing down every notion that ever was or ever will be but will not, the master magus is only in theory capable of comprehending and producing anything, but in actuality, they will not.
<br>
As the many biographies in this book will demonstrate, perhaps the only frontier we have yet to conquer is the human flaw itself - lapses in judgment, gaps in wisdom, poor luck, old age, ill health, and death - which prevent a magus from achieving the true omnipotence that the Sacred Opals they produce promise.
<br>
But with each life, as brief as it is in cosmic time, collective humanity moves ever closer to the true bounty which the natural world yet holds. Each attempt lays down a stone on our cobbled road to the stars.
<br>
This book will recount some of our most notable forerunners and the achievements which have inducted them into the ranks of masters.
<br>
Some may go on to greater heights after their first Sacred Opal - which are not the subject of this book - and some are merely content with the title, but each of them represents a moment where humanity rediscovers the universe, over and over, in humanity's flawed and human ways, of which there are as many as an Opal's light.
<br>
<!--lore note: producing the Sacred Opal is like creating a Turing-complete machine - a goal that does not do much by itself but opens up many possibilities.-->
$cssDesc[Flip through the book to read the entries.]}
]<!--end else (at preface)-->
]<currentEntry|

[
<br>
---
<ol>
<li>(link-rerun: "Flip to a random entry.")[{
    <!--all this code is to make sure: (1) At the beginning, there is a randomized but static reading order. (2) Flipping the pages proceed through that order. (3) The book remembers and displays the correct entry even after opening/closing some game menus.-->
    <!--initialize $stuff (only happens once when begin reading)-->
    (if: $stuff does not contain "readingOrder")[{
        <!--$stuff's readingOrder-->
        (set: $stuff's readingOrder to (shuffled: ...(range: 1, _SacredOpal's length)))
        <!--$stuff's readingTurn-->
        (set: $stuff's readingTurn to 0)
    }]
    <!--cycle through readingOrder-->
    (set: $stuff's readingTurn to it + 1)
    (if: $stuff's readingTurn > _SacredOpal's length)[(set: $stuff's readingTurn to 1)]
    <!--print content-->
    (replace: ?currentEntry)[{(print: _SacredOpal's ($stuff's readingOrder's ($stuff's readingTurn)))}]
    <!--scroll up to page's top-->
    <script>window.scrollTo(0,0)</script>
}]
<li>(link: "Read the preface again.")[{
    <!--clear $stuff-->
    (set: $stuff to (dm:))
    <!--refresh-->
    (redirect: (passage:)'s name)
}]
<li>(link: "Put the book away.")[{
    <!--clear $stuff-->
    (set: $stuff to (dm:))
    <!--goto-->
    (goto: $readingNav's before)}]
</ol>
]<quitReading|

}],

)